# {{ repo_name }}

{{ description }}

A **meta repo**: [mani](https://manicli.com) manages the sub-repos and their
cross-repo tasks, [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev) pins the tools and runs meta-level
tasks{% if use_fnox %}, and [fnox](https://github.com/jdx/fnox) holds the secrets{% endif %}.

## Setup

```sh
git init
git remote add origin https://github.com/{{ github_slug }}.git
git fetch
git checkout -f main
```

Install [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev), then the pinned tools:

```sh
curl https://mise.run | sh
mise install
```

Clone the sub-repos:

```sh
mise run sync
```

## Tasks

Tasks that run **inside each repo** are declared in `mani.yaml`; **meta-level**
tasks are scripts in `mise-tasks/`. `mise run` wraps the former so there's one
entry point, and `mise run tilt:up` shows every task as a button in a Tilt
dashboard.

| Task | Defined in | Does |
|------|-----------|------|
| `mise run sync` | mise | Clone sub-repos, update `.gitignore` **and** `.ignore` |
| `mise run hello` | mani | Print Hello World in every repo |
| `mise run status` | mani | `git status --short` across all sub-repos |
| `mise run pull` | mani | `git pull --ff-only` across all sub-repos |
| `mise run tasks` | mise | List the cross-repo tasks |
{%- if use_fnox %}
| `mise run secrets` | mise | List secrets in `fnox.toml` |
| `mise run secrets:check` | mise | Verify every secret resolves |
{%- endif %}

Because the cross-repo tasks are native mani tasks, you can also target and
parallelise them directly:

```sh
mani run status --all --parallel
mani run pull --tags active
mani run status --paths courses
mani tui
```

Add a new cross-repo task under `tasks:` in `mani.yaml` (`desc` + `cmd`); add a
meta-level one as an executable script in `.mise/tasks/` (`mise-tasks/` is kept
current by template updates, so don't edit it here).

## Adding a sub-repo

Add it under `projects:` in `mani.yaml`, then:

```sh
mise run sync
```

## Why both `.gitignore` and `.ignore`

`.gitignore` decides what **isn't committed**. `.ignore` decides what **isn't
searchable** in the editor. Both carry the same body plus the same `# mani #`
block, on the rule *what needn't be committed needn't be searchable*.

`.ignore` additionally opens with `!*` / `!*/` so the editor re-includes
everything git hid, before the shared body narrows it back down.

`mani sync` only maintains the block in `.gitignore` — `mise run sync` wraps it
and mirrors the block into `.ignore`, so use that rather than `mani sync`
directly.
{%- if use_fnox %}

## Secrets

`fnox.toml` is committed; values are encrypted at rest. Plaintext `.env` is
ignored by both files.

```sh
fnox set MY_SECRET value
fnox exec -- some-command   # runs with secrets in the environment
```
{%- endif %}

